lowing  municipalities to separate or opt out of  the school district system  when the discussion of the dollar loan  center as it's called now  uh came up and the deci the votes were  taken to eventually green light the  redevelopment of the pavilion  uh you talk about having people allow  them to have their say  those who were involved the rather vocal  opposition to that project  would suggest they didn't have their say  how do you address  their concerns that i acknowledge the  obviously the arena is being built now  but those are lingering concerns that  were brought up pretty significantly at  that time  so when those people came out i actually  went out and met with them in person i  walked the site with them  i listened to all of their concerns and  and the vast majority of the concerns  were  traffic potential traffic implications  the visibility that  the the building might impede their  visibility  open space and the availability to to  interact in the open space  and parking so all of those ideas i  brought back  and those were all incorporated with the  building design so  parking has been addressed there have  been more than a thousand parking spots  added to the site without disrupting any  of the existing parking  the building will sit actually nine feet  lower than the previous building  so that that that is not an issue the  dollar loan center  is one of many developments happening in  henderson there's the area around the  raiders  practice facility there's water street  which obviously as we were talking  before you are heavily involved in  uh there are some major developments on  the horizon how do you hope to or how do  you plan to if you're elected as mayor  continue that growth and and draw  more development into the city henderson  is very fortunate  in that we have people coming to us now  and so we can afford to be very choosy  about who we allow into the city and we  are looking for what's the highest and  best use for the  the limited space that we have available  and we have a very good vetting process  we have an  excellent economic development team a  great redevelopment team  you know everybody from community  development to fire to police are  looking at every project to make sure  that it  crosses all the teas and dots all the  eyes and meets all of the goals that we  have set for the city  to make sure that we're creating jobs  good paying jobs  that have minimal impacts on the  surrounding area what's your number one  priority if you're elected mayor number  one priority is public safety without  public safety we don't have anything  nobody's going to want to live here  nobody's going to want to send their  child to school  or open a business we've got to make  sure that we have public safety officers  in place  at enough levels to keep our city safe  now we have the full interview with  michelle romero on 8newsnow.com we delve  deeper into the issues of public safety  there was a big change at the police  chief position  not that long ago that's an ongoing  issue transparency we  visit the funding for the dollar loan  center and we're going to invite other  candidates onto our program as well  moving forward  now one other henderson council person  is also running for mayor that is dan  stewart  he announced his plans to run back in  april stewart's also a republican  although the position of mayor is  nonpartisan on the ballot  community safety and economic  development his priorities  before serving on the council stewart  had a career in the construction  industry  based on the last campaign finance  reports back in january stewart's raised  more money than romero 118 thousand  dollars last year while  romero has raised seven thousand five  hundred dollars  also in politics there may be a few  shakeups on the republican side of the  legislature  assemblyman andy matthews says he plans  to run for state controller he's in his  first term in the assembly  right now that position is held b